## **What is SQL\*Plus?**

**SQL\*Plus** is Oracle's command-line interface tool that allows you to interact directly with an Oracle database. Think of it as a terminal/console where you type commands and the database responds.

It is one of the oldest and most important tools in Oracle's ecosystem. Even though modern graphical tools exist today, SQL*Plus is still widely used by DBAs because:

- It is **always available** — it comes with every Oracle installation, no separate setup needed
- It works in **scripts and automation** — you can run files full of commands automatically
- It works even when **graphical tools fail** — if the network is down or GUI tools crash, SQL*Plus still works via direct server access
- It is **lightweight** — no heavy interface, just a terminal

**Important note:** SQL*Plus is specific to Oracle. Other DBMS have their own equivalents:

- MySQL has **mysql** command-line client
- PostgreSQL has **psql**
- SQL Server has **sqlcmd**

The concepts are the same — a text-based interface to run commands against the database.

---

## **How SQL\*Plus Works**

When you open SQL*Plus, it connects to an Oracle database instance and gives you a prompt:

text

```
SQL>

```

From here you can:

- Run **SQL statements** (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, CREATE, etc.)
- Run **PL/SQL blocks** (Oracle's procedural extension to SQL)
- Run **SQL\*Plus commands** (special commands only SQL*Plus understands, for formatting, settings, etc.)
- Run **script files** (**`.sql`** files containing multiple commands)

---

## **Starting SQL\*Plus**

### **Method 1 — Basic Connection (Local)**

bash

```
sqlplus username/password

```

Example:

bash

```
sqlplus system/mypassword

```

This connects to the **default database** on the local machine (defined by the **`ORACLE_SID`** environment variable).

### **Method 2 — Connect to a Specific Database (Remote or Local)**

bash

```
sqlplus username/password@database_alias

```

Example:

bash

```
sqlplus scott/tiger@orcl

```

Here **`orcl`** is the **TNS alias** — a name that maps to a specific database's host, port, and service name. This is defined in Oracle's **`tnsnames.ora`** file.

### **Method 3 — Connect Without Password in Command (Secure)**

bash

```
sqlplus /nolog

```

This starts SQL*Plus without connecting to any database. Then you connect inside:

text

```
SQL> CONNECT username/password@database

```

This method is preferred when you don't want your password visible in the terminal history.

### **Method 4 — OS Authentication (Sysdba)**

bash

```
sqlplus / as sysdba

```

This is a very powerful connection method. It connects you as **SYS** (the most privileged Oracle user) using **Operating System authentication** — meaning Oracle trusts you because you are already logged in as the Oracle OS user. No password needed.

DBAs use this to:

- Start and stop the database
- Perform recovery operations
- Access the database even when it is not fully open

---

## **Types of Users in SQL\*Plus Context**

When connecting, you specify a user. In Oracle there are special administrative users:

**SYS:**

- The most powerful user in Oracle
- Owns the **data dictionary** (all internal Oracle tables)
- Must connect **`AS SYSDBA`** or **`AS SYSOPER`**
- Used for startup, shutdown, recovery

**SYSTEM:**

- Second most powerful user
- Used for general DBA tasks
- Does not own the data dictionary
- Can connect normally without **`AS SYSDBA`**

**Regular Users (like SCOTT, HR):**

- Have limited privileges
- Can only access their own objects unless given permission

---

## **SQL\*Plus Commands vs SQL Commands**

This is a very important distinction that many students confuse.

### **SQL Commands**

These are standard database commands that are sent **to the database engine** for processing:

sql

```
SELECT * FROM employees;
CREATE TABLE students (id NUMBER, name VARCHAR2(50));
INSERT INTO products VALUES (1, 'Laptop', 75000);

```

These work in any Oracle interface, not just SQL*Plus.

### **SQL\*Plus Commands**

These are commands understood **only by SQL\*Plus itself**, not by the database. They control how SQL*Plus behaves:

text

```
SET PAGESIZE 50
SET LINESIZE 120
SPOOL output.txt
@myscript.sql
DESCRIBE employees

```

Let's go through the important ones in detail.

---

## **Important SQL\*Plus Commands**

### **1. CONNECT / CONN**

Connects to a database from within an active SQL*Plus session:

text

```
SQL> CONNECT hr/hrpassword@orcl

```

Or shorthand:

text

```
SQL> CONN hr/hrpassword@orcl

```

### **2. DISCONNECT**

Disconnects from the database but keeps SQL*Plus open:

text

```
SQL> DISCONNECT

```

### **3. EXIT / QUIT**

Disconnects and closes SQL*Plus completely:

text

```
SQL> EXIT
SQL> QUIT

```

### **4. DESCRIBE (DESC)**

Shows the **structure of a table or view** — its column names and data types:

text

```
SQL> DESCRIBE employees

```

Output would look like:

text

```
 Name                Null?    Type
 ------------------- -------- -------------------
 EMPLOYEE_ID         NOT NULL NUMBER(6)
 FIRST_NAME                   VARCHAR2(20)
 LAST_NAME           NOT NULL VARCHAR2(25)
 EMAIL               NOT NULL VARCHAR2(25)
 SALARY                       NUMBER(8,2)

```

This is one of the most frequently used commands when exploring an unfamiliar database.

### **5. SET Commands**

These control how output is displayed:

text

```
SQL> SET PAGESIZE 50

```

Sets how many lines appear per page before headers repeat. Default is 14, which is too small. DBAs usually set it to 50 or higher.

text

```
SQL> SET LINESIZE 150

```

Sets the width of each line in characters. If your columns are wide, increase this to avoid ugly line wrapping.

text

```
SQL> SET ECHO ON

```

When running script files, this makes SQL*Plus print each command before executing it. Good for debugging.

text

```
SQL> SET FEEDBACK OFF

```

Turns off the "5 rows selected." type messages. Useful in scripts where you want clean output.

text

```
SQL> SET HEADING OFF

```

Removes column headers from query output. Useful when the output will be processed by another program.

text

```
SQL> SET SERVEROUTPUT ON

```

**This one is very important.** It enables output from **`DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE`** — Oracle's way of printing messages from PL/SQL code. Without this, your PL/SQL programs appear to do nothing.

### **6. SPOOL**

This command **captures everything displayed in SQL\*Plus into a file**:

text

```
SQL> SPOOL /home/oracle/output.txt
SQL> SELECT * FROM employees;
SQL> SPOOL OFF

```

Everything between **`SPOOL filename`** and **`SPOOL OFF`** gets saved to the file. This is very useful for:

- Saving query results
- Logging what happened during a maintenance session
- Creating reports

### **7. @ (Run a Script File)**

The **`@`** symbol runs a **`.sql`** script file:

text

```
SQL> @/home/oracle/scripts/create_tables.sql

```

Or for a file in the current directory:

text

```
SQL> @create_tables.sql

```

This is how DBAs run large sets of commands without typing them one by one. You write all your SQL in a text file and run it with one command.

### **8. @@ (Run a Script Relative to Current Script)**

When a script calls other scripts, **`@@`** looks for the second script in the **same directory as the calling script**, not the current working directory. Used inside script files.

### **9. START**

Same as **`@`** — runs a script file:

text

```
SQL> START /path/to/script.sql

```

### **10. SAVE**

Saves the last command you typed into a file:

text

```
SQL> SAVE myquery.sql

```

### **11. GET**

Loads a file into the SQL*Plus buffer (ready to run) without running it:

text

```
SQL> GET myquery.sql

```

### **12. EDIT**

Opens the current buffer in a text editor so you can modify the last command:

text

```
SQL> EDIT

```

On Linux this opens **`vi`** by default. On Windows it opens Notepad.

### **13. LIST (L)**

Shows the contents of the current SQL buffer (the last command):

text

```
SQL> L

```

### **14. RUN (/)**

Runs whatever is currently in the SQL buffer:

text

```
SQL> /

```

The **`/`** on its own line is used constantly — after editing or retrieving a query, you type **`/`** to run it.

### **15. CLEAR BUFFER**

Clears the current SQL buffer:

text

```
SQL> CLEAR BUFFER

```

### **16. SHOW**

Displays current SQL*Plus settings:

text

```
SQL> SHOW ALL
SQL> SHOW PAGESIZE
SQL> SHOW USER

```

**`SHOW USER`** tells you which user you are currently connected as — very useful to confirm you're in the right account before running destructive commands.

### **17. COLUMN (COL)**

Formats how a specific column appears in output:

text

```
SQL> COLUMN first_name FORMAT A20
SQL> COLUMN salary FORMAT 999,999.99

```

**`A20`** means character column, 20 characters wide.\
**`999,999.99`** formats a number with commas and 2 decimal places.

This makes output much more readable.

### **18. TTITLE and BTITLE**

Sets a title that appears at the **top** or **bottom** of each page of output:

text

```
SQL> TTITLE 'Employee Report'
SQL> BTITLE 'Confidential'

```

Used when generating formal reports.

---

## **Running Scripts at Login — login.sql**

SQL*Plus looks for a file called **`login.sql`** in the current directory when it starts. If found, it runs it automatically. DBAs use this to set their preferred settings every time:

sql

```
SET PAGESIZE 100
SET LINESIZE 200
SET SERVEROUTPUT ON
SET TIME ON
SHOW USER

```

**`SET TIME ON`** displays the current time in the SQL*Plus prompt, which is useful for timing queries.

---

## **The SQL Buffer**

SQL*Plus maintains a **buffer** — a temporary memory area holding the last SQL command you ran. Understanding this is important:

- Every time you type and run a SQL command, it goes into the buffer
- SQL*Plus commands (like SET, SPOOL) do **not** go into the buffer
- You can recall and re-run the buffer with **`/`**
- You can edit the buffer with **`EDIT`**
- You can save it with **`SAVE`**

---

## **Substitution Variables**

SQL*Plus supports **variables** that prompt the user for input when a script runs:

### **Single Ampersand (&) — Prompts every time it appears:**

sql

```
SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department_id = &dept_id;

```

When you run this, SQL*Plus asks:

text

```
Enter value for dept_id: 10

```

### **Double Ampersand (&&) — Prompts once and remembers the value:**

sql

```
SELECT &&column_name FROM employees;

```

### **DEFINE — Set a variable manually:**

text

```
SQL> DEFINE dept = 20
SQL> SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department_id = &dept;

```

### **ACCEPT — A more controlled way to accept user input:**

text

```
SQL> ACCEPT v_name PROMPT 'Enter employee name: '
SQL> SELECT * FROM employees WHERE last_name = '&v_name';

```

This is useful for creating **interactive scripts** that non-technical users can run.

---

## **SQLPLUS and DBA Tasks**

As a DBA, you'll use SQL*Plus for important administrative commands:

### **Startup and Shutdown (covered in detail later):**

sql

```
STARTUP
SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE

```

### **Checking Database Status:**

sql

```
SELECT STATUS FROM V$INSTANCE;
SELECT NAME, OPEN_MODE FROM V$DATABASE;

```

### **Viewing Alert Log Location:**

sql

```
SELECT VALUE FROM V$DIAG_INFO WHERE NAME = 'Diag Trace';

```

### **Granting Privileges:**

sql

```
GRANT CREATE TABLE TO hr;
GRANT DBA TO new_admin;

```

---

## **SQL\*Plus in MySQL and PostgreSQL Context**

To reinforce that these concepts apply beyond Oracle:

**MySQL equivalent (mysql client):**

bash

```
mysql -u root -p
mysql -u root -p mydatabase

```

Inside mysql client:

text

```
mysql> SHOW DATABASES;
mysql> USE employees;
mysql> DESCRIBE staff;
mysql> SOURCE /path/to/script.sql;
mysql> STATUS;

```

**PostgreSQL equivalent (psql):**

bash

```
psql -U postgres
psql -U postgres -d mydb

```

Inside psql:

text

```
postgres=# \l          -- list databases
postgres=# \c mydb     -- connect to database
postgres=# \d tablename -- describe table
postgres=# \i script.sql -- run script file
postgres=# \q          -- quit

```

The idea is the same — a command-line client where you type commands and interact with the database.

---

## **Summary Table**

{% table %}
- **Command**
- **Purpose**
---
- **`sqlplus / as sysdba`**
- Connect as most privileged user
---
- **`CONNECT user/pass@db`**
- Connect to a database
---
- **`DESCRIBE tablename`**
- Show table structure
---
- **`SET PAGESIZE n`**
- Control output page size
---
- **`SET LINESIZE n`**
- Control output line width
---
- **`SET SERVEROUTPUT ON`**
- Enable PL/SQL output
---
- **`SPOOL file`**
- Save output to file
---
- **`@script.sql`**
- Run a script file
---
- **`SHOW USER`**
- Display current user
---
- **`COLUMN col FORMAT`**
- Format column output
---
- **`/`**
- Re-run last command
---
- **`EXIT`**
- Close SQL*Plus
{% /table %}

